On 8/15/2016 4:18 PM, Bruno Panasiewicz wrote:
> That is or isn't the point, depending on the intentions of the
> proposers. I think that the main motive is to free up a spot in the now
> expensive short cmavo space. Simply, the uses of =C2=ABli=C2=BB and =C2=
=ABcu=C2=BB have no
> overlaps, so collapsing them is a good idea.

[large number of strongly negative attitudinals]

Why does "having no overlaps" make it "a good idea"?  The language is=20
Lojban, not Speedtalk".  And why those particular words; they are hardly=20
the least used short words in the language?

If one were going to collapse two words, one would probably want them as=20
similar as possible in meaning and usage..

This would be an incredibly bad idea, if only because of the relearning=20
penalty and the invalidation of almost thirty years of language use.  It=20
is that sort of arbitrary prescriptive change that I have fought most=20
strongly against.

And why is it necessary to free up a spot?  We are supposedly done with=20
the language design.  There should be no new cmavo unless they arise=20
through actual usage, and it is rather hard to imagine a usage arising=20
so useful as to suddenly and arbitrarily demand a short wordform.=20
Especially since no such need has arisen in 30 years.

But if such occurred there are other choices far better than jamming two=20
entirely unrelated functions into one word (something that was=20
explicitly designed OUT of the language).  Move a little-use 2 letter=20
cmavo out of that word space (or even eliminate it; e.g. de and di could=20
be replaced by da with a subscript).

Seriously considering this sort of change at this stage of the language,=20
is a good way to get lojbab to retire immediately and permanently.

lojbab


